+# Daily Grind origin migration
+
+This static project now builds **two deployment directories**. No deployment is performed by the build. The checked-in origins use reserved `.example` addresses because the repository contained no production addresses, manifest, or saved-settings implementation.
+
+1. Set `oldOrigin` and `newOrigin` in `migration.config.json` to the real HTTPS origins (no trailing slash). Alternatively set `OLD_ORIGIN` and `NEW_ORIGIN` during the build. Both origins must share a registrable domain; private suffixes such as `github.io` are checked too.
+2. Set `oldAppId` to the installed application's **existing manifest ID**, preserving it exactly. Its default is `/`, matching an app whose original start URL was `/`. Set `newAppId` to the destination ID. Do not rename an existing app's manifest URL: if it differs from `/manifest.webmanifest`, serve the generated manifest at its existing URL and update the HTML link accordingly.
+3. List the application's localStorage configuration keys in `configurationKeys`. The defaults are `daily-grind:preferences` and `daily-grind:settings`; this repository previously stored no configuration. Only listed keys transfer. Do not include authentication tokens or unrelated storage.
+4. Run `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`, `pnpm test`, and `pnpm build`.
+5. Deploy **dist/old/** at the old origin first, then **dist/new/** at the new origin. Serve `manifest.webmanifest` as `application/manifest+json` and `/.well-known/web-app-origin-association` as `application/json`. Include the hidden `.well-known` directory in the deployment. Keep the old manifest at its original URL and preserve its existing identity/icons/shortcuts when integrating with an existing installation.
+
+The old manifest advertises `migrate_to`; the new manifest declares `migrate_from`. The old origin's association file authorizes the exact destination app ID. Compatible browsers offer an installation update that retains OS integration. Other browsers get an ordinary link and instructions to install from their browser menu; automatic shortcut preservation is unavailable there. Browser consent is still required.
+
+Settings transfer independently when the new page opens, using an old-origin iframe and `postMessage` with exact origin, source-window, and request-nonce checks. Existing destination values win; source values are never deleted. Completion is recorded only after all writes succeed. Blocked storage, errors, and timeouts show a retry action without marking the transfer complete. Preference consumers should read storage after migration (or on the next page load).
+
+Keep the old origin and `/migration/bridge.html` reachable throughout the transition; do not redirect them to the new origin. If the hosting platform sends CSP, allow the new origin in the bridge's `frame-ancestors`, allow the old origin in the new page's `frame-src`, and do not send `X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN` or `DENY` for the bridge. Old-origin storage must remain accessible in the same-site frame. Test failure states before retiring the old deployment.
+
+Cookies, IndexedDB, login sessions, and browser permissions are not transferred by this implementation. They require application-specific synchronization or renewed permission. The repository contains no such data stores. The manifest mechanism itself transfers installation state, not origin storage or permission grants.
+
+Checks use Node tests and static build output only; no server or browser is required.
+
+References: [Chrome migration guide](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/modern-web-guidance/guides/pwa/migrate-web-app-origin), [origin comparison guidance](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/modern-web-guidance/guides/security/validate-origins).
